  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nConsistent and strong ridging continues to dominate western and\ncentral Wyoming through the rest of the upcoming week. This will\nkeep the very warm and dry conditions going, with highs 10 to 15\ndegrees above normal through Friday. Those leaves that are\nalready changing colors are going to get confused for a while.\nThere is a weak and dissipating midlevel trough moving out of\nthe Great Basin into western Colorado, which may push a few\nvirga showers into far southern WY this afternoon, but with\nsurface dewpoint temps in the 20s, any precip that tries to fall\nwill quickly evaporate well before reaching the ground.\n\nHints of a larger change start to show on Friday, as a larger\ntrough starts to drop southward along the Pacific Coast. This\nwill start to tighten up the surface gradient in the region, \nbringing a more typical windy Wyoming day. This along with the \nstill very dry conditions will lead to near critical fire \nweather concerns during the afternoon. This pattern continues \ninto the day Saturday as well. Longer term models still can't \nquite agree on the timing of the arrival of the trough this \nweekend, but are becoming more consistent in bringing it through\nnonetheless. Confidence in increasing in a considerable weather\nchange with this system, bringing much colder temperatures, \nample rainfall at lower levels, and the first decent amount of \nsnowfall for the main mountain ranges across NW WY.",
